  <div class="page-break"></div>

  <div class="print-section">
    <h2>Overview</h2>
    <p>Thanksgiving spread across two days. Turkey gets marinated in shio-koji (Japanese fermented rice) for extra flavor and tenderness. Thursday is prep and a light Japanese-fusion dinner. Friday is the full feast.</p>

    <h3>Why Koji?</h3>
    <p>Shio-koji is fermented rice with salt. The enzymes break down proteins, which makes meat more tender and adds a lot of umami flavor. It's basically a marinade that does more work than regular salt water.</p>

    <h4>The Process:</h4>
    <ul>
      <li>Made shio-koji 6 days ahead (kept it in a sous-vide bath at 91°F to speed it up)</li>
      <li>Butchered turkey into parts, coated in koji, vacuum sealed for 36 hours</li>
      <li>Wiped it off, let the skin air-dry in the fridge for 36 hours</li>
      <li>Roast on Friday</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Why Two Days?</h3>
    <p>The two-day thing makes it less chaotic. Stock is done and cold when I need it for gravy. Bread is already baked. Dessert is made. Turkey just needs to roast.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="page-break"></div>

  <div class="print-section">
    <h2>Complete Timeline</h2>
    
    <h3>Sunday, November 23rd</h3>
    <p><strong>Evening:</strong> Start thawing turkey in fridge</p>
    <p><strong>Midnight:</strong> Start shio-koji in sous-vide bath at 91°F (72 hours)</p>

    <h3>Monday-Tuesday, November 24-25</h3>
    <p>Shio-koji continues fermenting. Turkey continues thawing.</p>

    <h3>Tuesday Midnight, November 25→26</h3>
    <p><strong>Butcher turkey:</strong> Separate breasts, legs, wings, backbone</p>
    <p><strong>Apply koji marinade:</strong> 2-3 tbsp per pound, vacuum seal, refrigerate 36 hours</p>

    <h3>Wednesday Midnight, November 26→27</h3>
    <p><strong>Remove from koji:</strong> Pat off excess, wipe clean</p>
    <p><strong>Start air-drying:</strong> Wire rack in fridge, uncovered, 36 hours</p>

    <h3>Thursday, November 27 - Thanksgiving Day</h3>
    <p><strong>Morning/Afternoon Prep:</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li>Bake milk bread dinner rolls</li>
      <li>Make cranberry orange compote</li>
      <li>Bake eggnog Basque cheesecake</li>
      <li>Roast carcass parts, make turkey stock (3-4 hours)</li>
    </ul>
    <p><strong>Evening Dinner:</strong> Japanese-American fusion meal (soba, edamame, salad)</p>

    <h3>Friday, November 28 - Turkey Day!</h3>
    <p><strong>10:00 AM:</strong> Remove turkey from fridge, prep roasting setup</p>
    <p><strong>12:00 PM:</strong> Turkey in oven (legs → breasts, stagger timing)</p>
    <p><strong>1:30 PM:</strong> Rest turkey, make gravy</p>
    <p><strong>2:00 PM:</strong> EAT!</p>
  </div>

