  <div class="page-break"></div>

  <div class="print-section">
    <h2>Thursday: Thanksgiving Day Details</h2>

    <h3>Milk Bread Dinner Rolls</h3>
    <p>Mix, knead, first rise → shape into rolls → second rise → egg wash → bake until golden → cool and store</p>

    <h3>Cranberry Orange Compote</h3>
    <p>Combine cranberries, orange zest, juice, and sugar in saucepan. Simmer 10-15 minutes until berries burst. Cool and refrigerate.</p>

    <h3>Eggnog Basque Cheesecake</h3>
    <p>Beat cream cheese smooth → add sugar, then eggs → mix in cream, eggnog, nutmeg, vanilla → pour into parchment-lined pan → bake 400-425°F for burnt top → cool, then refrigerate overnight</p>

    <h3>Turkey Stock (Koji-Enhanced)</h3>
    <p>Roast koji-soaked carcass parts until golden → transfer to stock pot with aromatics → simmer 3-4 hours → strain, cool, refrigerate</p>

    <h3>Evening: Japanese-American Fusion Dinner</h3>
    <p><strong>Soba Bowl:</strong> Ladle in-progress stock, boost with dashi, add mushrooms and peas, top cooked soba noodles</p>
    <p><strong>Edamame:</strong> Steam, toss with sesame oil + maple + soy or other fusion spices</p>
    <p><strong>Salad:</strong> Mixed greens, Japanese dressing, crispy turkey skin croutons</p>
  </div>

  <div class="page-break"></div>

  <div class="print-section">
    <h2>Friday: Turkey Day Execution</h2>

    <h3>Roasting the Turkey</h3>
    <p><strong>Setup:</strong> Wire rack on sheet pan, water in bottom, predictive thermometer ready</p>
    <p><strong>Dark Meat:</strong> Legs in first, target 185°F</p>
    <p><strong>White Meat:</strong> Breasts timed to finish at 155°F when legs hit 185°F</p>

    <h3>Making the Gravy</h3>
    <p>Remove turkey to rest → deglaze roasting pan on stovetop → add cold turkey stock → build gravy with hybrid thickener system:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Roux:</strong> Traditional flavor base</li>
      <li><strong>Potato starch:</strong> Clean, glossy thickness</li>
      <li><strong>Gelatin:</strong> Body and mouthfeel</li>
      <li><strong>Xanthan:</strong> Consistency and stability</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="page-break"></div>

  <div class="print-section">
    <h2>Leftovers Pie</h2>

    <h3>The Concept</h3>
    <p>One layered dish instead of multiple containers. Everything melds together, gravy soaks through, complete Thanksgiving in every bite.</p>

    <h3>The Structure</h3>
    <p><strong>Bottom:</strong> Stuffing (absorbs gravy, provides structure)</p>
    <p><strong>Middle:</strong> Turkey + Brussels sprouts + other sides + gravy (the glue)</p>
    <p><strong>Top:</strong> Hasselback potatoes (the "crust")</p>

    <h3>Assembly</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Choose deep casserole dish or pie plate</li>
      <li>Pack stuffing firmly in bottom (1-2 inches)</li>
      <li>Add turkey pieces evenly</li>
      <li>Distribute Brussels sprouts and sides</li>
      <li>Pour gravy generously over everything</li>
      <li>Top with potatoes, slightly overlapping</li>
      <li>Cover tightly, refrigerate</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Reheating</h3>
    <p><strong>Oven:</strong> Cover with foil, 350°F for 30-40 minutes → remove foil, broil 2-3 minutes</p>
    <p><strong>Microwave:</strong> Cut portion, microwave 2-3 minutes, check, continue until hot</p>
    <p><strong>Serve with:</strong> Cranberry orange compote on the side</p>
  </div>

